{
  "text": " So the vaccines were overpromised and under-delivered in terms of what they could accomplish. You'll remember when they were first rolled out, people were told, this will not only prevent you from getting sick, Tucker, but it'll prevent you from getting grandma sick. It'll prevent, it'll protect other people, right? That kind of argument would hold more force if we had what's called a sterilizing vaccine for COVID. A sterilizing vaccine like the measles vaccine prevents me from getting measles, but also prevents me from getting a low-level infection that I can transmit to someone else. We now know, and we knew pretty early on, certainly by the time I filed my lawsuit in August, that none of the COVID vaccines offer sterilizing immunity. You can still get infected, you can still transmit the virus to others. So the whole argument that you should do it for the sake of other people really falls apart when applied to these specific vaccines. So we fall back on, okay, you're assuming the risks of getting vaccinated or not getting vaccinated. You're assuming the benefits of getting vaccinated or not getting vaccinated. So given that the risks and benefits are crude to you, you should have the right to decide which risks you want to assume and which risks you don't want to assume.",
